// expect-exit: 94
//
// `Vec<T>` — OUT OF RANGE: `vec_reserve` on a FREED `Vec`. RX-139.
//
// **BEFORE THE FIX THIS PROGRAM DID NOT TERMINATE**, which is the only reason
// it is the first of the four. `vec_free` sets `cap = 0` deliberately, as
// poison; `vec_reserve` then read `int64:nc = v.cap` and ran
// `while (nc < want) { nc = nc * 2i64; }`, which multiplies zero by two
// forever. Measured against the shipped file: `timeout 6` → **exit 124**.
//
// A NON-TERMINATING LOOP IN THE CONTAINER EVERY ENGINE IS BUILT ON is the exact
// failure mode `CLAUDE.md`'s first non-negotiable rule exists to prevent — a
// search is `O(m·n)` always, because catastrophic backtracking is a denial of
// service and the language has no cancellation (D-062) with which to survive
// one. This one is reached with no backtracking at all.
//
// The harness judges by exit code and a hang is not an exit code, so a
// regression here shows up as the runner's own timeout rather than as a wrong
// number. That is worth knowing before reading a red.
//
// ONE CASE PER FILE, because a trapping call cannot be followed by an assertion.
mod:vec_oob_reserve_freed;

use "../../src/core/vec.npk".*;

func:main = int32(cstring[]:argv) {
    // Derived from `argv` so nothing folds: `argv.len` is 1 with no arguments.
    int64:one = argv.len;

    Vec<int64>:v = raw vec_init::<int64>(4i64);
    drop vec_push(@v, 11i64);
    // The poison is asserted rather than assumed: if `vec_free` ever stops
    // setting `cap = 0`, this file is testing something else and says so with
    // its own code instead of passing for the wrong reason.
    drop vec_free(@v);
    if (v.cap != 0i64) { exit 30i32; }

    drop vec_reserve(@v, one);

    // Unreachable. A DIFFERENT code from the trap, so "the guard was removed"
    // and "the guard fired" can never be confused.
    exit 50i32;
};

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
